  {
    "id": 38,
    "slug": "website-maintenance-plans-cost",
    "title": "Website Maintenance Plans: What They Cost and What You Get",
    "category": "Hosting & Management",
    "location": "US & Global",
    "date": "October 2026",
    "readTime": "6 min read",
    "author": "Sauranahmed Mansuri",
    "authorRole": "Head of Technology",
    "summary": "What do website maintenance plans cost, and what should they include? A plain-English guide for small business owners.",
    "faqs": [{"q":"How much does website maintenance cost per month?","a":"Basic plans typically start around $50 to $150 per month, mid-level plans around $150 to $500, and advanced plans for complex sites or web apps can cost more."},{"q":"Do I really need a maintenance plan?","a":"Websites need regular updates, backups, and monitoring to stay secure and fast. You can handle this yourself, but a plan saves time and reduces the risk of downtime or security problems."},{"q":"Can a company maintain a website it did not build?","a":"Often yes. A good provider will audit the existing site first, fix urgent issues, and then take over regular maintenance."}],
    "content": "<p>Launching a website is not the finish line. Software ages, security threats change, and small problems pile up quietly until something breaks at the worst moment. A website maintenance plan is how businesses keep their site fast, secure, and current without having to think about it every week.</p>\n\n  <h2>What is a website maintenance plan?</h2>\n  <p>It is a monthly or annual service where a developer or studio looks after your website on an ongoing basis. Instead of paying for emergency fixes, you pay a predictable fee for regular care and quick help when you need it.</p>\n\n  <h2>What a good plan should include</h2>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Updates:</strong> keeping software, libraries, and dependencies current to close security gaps.</li>\n    <li><strong>Automated backups:</strong> regular, encrypted backups stored separately so you can recover quickly.</li>\n    <li><strong>Uptime monitoring:</strong> alerts when your site goes down, so problems are fixed before customers notice.</li>\n    <li><strong>Security monitoring:</strong> protection against bots, spam, and common attacks.</li>\n    <li><strong>Performance checks:</strong> reviewing speed and Core Web Vitals so the site stays fast.</li>\n    <li><strong>Content updates:</strong> a set number of text, image, or page changes each month.</li>\n    <li><strong>Direct support:</strong> a real person who responds when you have a question or issue.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h2>Typical maintenance costs in the US</h2>\n  <p>These are common market ranges, and actual pricing varies by provider and site complexity.</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Basic care (about $50 to $150 per month):</strong> backups, updates, uptime monitoring, and light support for a simple brochure site.</li>\n    <li><strong>Standard care (about $150 to $500 per month):</strong> everything in basic, plus security monitoring, performance work, and regular content changes.</li>\n    <li><strong>Advanced care ($500+ per month):</strong> for web applications, booking systems, or e-commerce sites that need engineering support, feature work, and faster response times.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>Hosting is sometimes bundled and sometimes billed separately, so always ask.</p>\n\n  <h2>What happens if you skip maintenance?</h2>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Security risk:</strong> outdated software is a common way sites get compromised.</li>\n    <li><strong>Slower pages:</strong> unchecked performance drift costs you visitors and search visibility.</li>\n    <li><strong>Broken features:</strong> forms, booking, and checkout can quietly fail after updates elsewhere.</li>\n    <li><strong>Expensive emergencies:</strong> fixing a hacked or crashed site costs more than prevention.</li>\n    <li><strong>Lost data:</strong> without backups, a single failure can erase months of work.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h2>DIY vs. a maintenance plan</h2>\n  <p>You can maintain a site yourself if you are comfortable with updates, backups, and troubleshooting, and you have the time. Most owners would rather spend that time running their business. A plan trades a modest monthly fee for peace of mind and a faster path to fixes.</p>\n\n  <h2>Questions to ask before you sign</h2>\n  <ol>\n    <li>What exactly is included each month, and what costs extra?</li>\n    <li>How quickly do you respond to urgent issues?</li>\n    <li>Where are backups stored, and how often are they taken?</li>\n    <li>Can I cancel anytime, and do I keep my code and accounts?</li>\n    <li>Will I work directly with the engineers or go through a middleman?</li>\n    <li>Do you offer a monthly summary of what was done?</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n  <h3>How much does website maintenance cost per month?</h3>\n  <p>Basic plans typically start around $50 to $150 per month, mid-level plans around $150 to $500, and advanced plans for complex sites or web apps can cost more.</p>\n  <h3>Do I really need a maintenance plan?</h3>\n  <p>Websites need regular updates, backups, and monitoring to stay secure and fast. You can handle this yourself, but a plan saves time and reduces the risk of downtime or security problems.</p>\n  <h3>Can a company maintain a website it did not build?</h3>\n  <p>Often yes. A good provider will audit the existing site first, fix urgent issues, and then take over regular maintenance.</p>\n\n  <h2>Keep your website healthy</h2>\n  <p>Caventra&rsquo;s <a href=\"/services/maintenance/\">management and care plans</a> include updates, backups, and direct engineer support, and we also offer <a href=\"/services/security/\">security</a> and <a href=\"/services/hosting/\">managed hosting</a>. We can maintain sites we did not build too. <a href=\"/contact/\">Contact us</a> and we will review your site.</p>"
  }
